# Release-path ownership. Every path here can change what a published binary is,
# where it comes from, or who may sign it, so each needs review from a release
# signer, not just any writer.
#
# No catch-all `*` line: branch protection on `main` requires code-owner review,
# so a `*` line would make these three people a bottleneck on every change.
.github/workflows/   @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
.github/actions/     @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
.github/CODEOWNERS   @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/release-signing/ @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
scripts/             @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The packages that run inside those same jobs, and the config that decides what
# gets released. They have the same reach as the scripts above, so the same bar:
#
#   - `bun.lock` chooses the code that runs beside the signing identity and the
#     store token. The release lane runs a frozen install and then a publish script
#     that imports `@vercel/blob`. No lifecycle scripts run, so a payload would have
#     to be in imported code, which is what this file selects.
#   - `package.json` `dependencies` are compiled into the shipped binary by
#     `bun build --compile` (src/api.ts imports zod), then hashed and signed. Bad
#     bytes there would be the genuine release, so job isolation does not help and
#     review is the only control.
#   - The release-please files decide the version and the tag that
#     `release-cli.yml` builds from.
#
# Leading slashes matter: CODEOWNERS uses gitignore-style patterns, so a bare
# `package.json` would match at any depth, not just the repository root.
/package.json                  @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/bun.lock                      @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/release-please-config.json    @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
/.release-please-manifest.json @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The self-update decision path. `src/release-signing/` decides whether a signed
# build record verifies. These two decide what is done with that result:
#
#   - `src/update.ts` turns a verify failure into a refusal. Changing one error
#     path from "refuse to install" to "warn and continue" defeats the signature
#     check without touching the verifier, and looks like ordinary error handling.
#   - `src/ring-follow.ts` decides whose bytes an install accepts. Self-update
#     compares checksums, not versions, so the recorded pointer is the whole policy
#     for what a machine downloads. Treating a missing record as a refusal, not a
#     guessed default, is what stops an install following a ring nobody chose.
#
# SECURITY.md puts "the self-update path, including signature verification and
# how an update decides what to install" in scope, so both halves are owned.
src/update.ts      @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/ring-follow.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The credential path, in scope per SECURITY.md: "credential handling, the
# OAuth + PKCE login flow, token storage on disk". Each file holds a control that
# is easy to miss in review and easy to remove with a small diff:
#
#   - `src/oauth.ts` makes the token request and receives the access, refresh and
#     id tokens. It is the only place credentials exist before they are stored,
#     and it must stay Auth Code + PKCE (never ROPC).
#   - `src/pkce.ts` generates the verifier and the CSRF `state`. Their entropy and
#     the S256 challenge bind the authorizing browser to the redeeming process;
#     shortening a length or returning a fixed value makes the flow interceptable.
#   - `src/loopback.ts` decides which callback requests may complete a login
#     (origin first, then `state`). Relaxing either lets another local process
#     inject an authorization code or cancel a login.
#   - `src/paste-callback.ts` handles the SSH flow, where the callback URL is
#     pasted by hand through the same logic, so it is part of the same control.
#   - `src/storage.ts` writes the 0600 token file. The chmod after the write is
#     needed because `mode` applies only on creation; removing it leaves tokens
#     readable by every user, and no test output changes.
#   - `src/config-dir.ts` creates the 0700 directory above it. Whichever command
#     runs first on a fresh machine sets its mode, so a change here can weaken
#     token storage from a command unrelated to tokens.
src/oauth.ts          @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/pkce.ts           @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/loopback.ts       @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/paste-callback.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/storage.ts        @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/config-dir.ts     @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The two files that decide where a credential goes and whether it may travel in
# cleartext. Neither is purely a security file, but both are owned:
#
#   - `src/target.ts` picks the backend at run time from a flag or env var, so it
#     names the host that receives the access token. Changing its prod default, or
#     the endpoints a target implies, redirects live credentials without touching
#     any file that mentions a token.
#   - `src/config.ts` holds `assertSecureUrl`, which refuses a plaintext endpoint
#     except on loopback, so auth codes and refresh tokens never go over
#     `http://`. `update.ts` also uses it to check the recorded install URL, so it
#     is on both paths. The ordinary config churn is accepted as the cost.
src/target.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/config.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The agent's instructions. `skills/wego/SKILL.md` is compiled into the binary
# (`src/skill-embed.ts`) and, after every self-update, written into each agent
# skill directory the machine has (`skill install --owned-only`). The user's
# coding agent follows it with the user's permissions, so a sentence added here
# reaches every install like code does, and is easier to miss in review.
#
#   - `skills/` is the body itself.
#   - `plugin/` is what the promote lane publishes to wego/skills.
#   - `src/skill.ts` decides WHERE the body is written (the agent path table).
#   - `src/skill-embed.ts` decides WHICH body is embedded.
#
# The body ships only inside the signed binary, so signing covers delivery. As
# with `package.json` above, the bytes are the genuine release, and review is
# the only control.
skills/            @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
plugin/            @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/skill.ts       @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego
src/skill-embed.ts @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The declared scope. The entries above cite SECURITY.md as their reason, so
# narrowing its scope needs the same review.
/SECURITY.md @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The input to the API contract check. Owned for review visibility, not runtime
# reach.
#
# Nothing in this file executes. `scripts/generate-api-types.ts` reads it at
# build time to emit `src/api-types.d.ts`, which is not committed, is imported
# only with `import type`, and never reaches the shipped binary. The endpoints
# the CLI talks to come from `src/target.ts`, owned above.
#
# It does control the contract check itself. The generator and
# `scripts/ci-contract-drift.test.ts` are owned, so the document they check
# against should be too:
#
#   - Checks A and C (`src/api-contract.ts`, under `bun run typecheck`) compare
#     the CLI's Zod types against this file. Widening a request schema here
#     makes Check C stop objecting to a field the real API would reject.
#   - Check B (`src/api-contract.test.ts`) walks this file to confirm the fields
#     the CLI depends on are still published. An edit here can make it pass for
#     a path the API does not serve.
#   - The CI drift step only warns (the API deploys on its own schedule), so a
#     hand-edited contract merges with a `::warning::`.
#
# The file is large, so a changed enum, a relaxed `required` or a dropped field
# is easy to miss. A refresh commit changes only this file
# (`bun run api-contract:refresh`, per CONTRIBUTING), so the owner only has to
# ask whether it is the generator's output.
#
# Low urgency: no credential or executable path goes through here. The worst
# case is a weaker compile-time check.
contract/openapi.json @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

# The integration tier. Unlike the unit tests, it is a release gate: `release`
# needs `integration (<target>)` on every target but Windows. Two parts can make
# that gate meaningless or unsafe with a small diff:
#
#   - `harness/contract.ts` and `harness/fake.ts` hold every exchange to the
#     contract. Relaxing the validator, or letting an unexpected request pass,
#     leaves every scenario green while checking nothing.
#   - `fixtures/` is what the fake answers with. A fixture copied from a real
#     response must not carry the account it came from; review is the check.
integration/ @sunny-wego @yeouchien-wego @chuyeowego

